course: politics
lesson: 1
title: What politics is: deciding things together
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Politics is how a group of people decides things that everybody then has to live with, when people want different things and there is only one ball, one pot of money or one set of rules.
explain: Picture lunchtime with one football and two games that both want it: the children shout, then vote, and the bigger group gets the ball; a country does the same thing with bigger questions, like hospitals, roads and taxes.
explain: Families do small politics too: Dad wants the beach, Gran wants the mountains, and a deal like a beach near a theme park gives everyone a bit of what they wanted.
explain: People give reasons, listen, argue, vote, and sometimes protest peacefully; in a democracy the voters choose the people who make the rules, so rules are made by people and people can change them.
explain: Grown-ups get angry about politics because it decides money, safety and fairness, and they value these differently; you can disagree strongly and still be kind, and you can check what each politician actually did instead of believing "they're all liars".
fact: Politics is how groups of people make decisions and share things out when they cannot all have what they want.
fact: A compromise is a deal where each side gets part of what it wants; to negotiate is to talk until you find such a deal.
fact: A town or city council has a limited budget, so spending money on one thing, like a library, means less for something else, like a swimming pool.
fact: Taxes are money people and businesses must pay to the government; they pay for public services such as schools, hospitals, roads and police.
fact: People disagree about how much tax there should be and who should pay most, which is one reason tax makes people cross.
fact: A candidate is a person trying to be chosen; voters often ask whether a candidate's promise can really happen, not just whether it sounds nice.
fact: In a democracy people choose their leaders by voting, and those leaders make the laws, so a law people dislike can be changed.
fact: A protest is when people gather to show they want something changed; in many countries peaceful protest is allowed, and people argue about its limits, such as blocking roads.
fact: Policies about bins, buses and prices are political decisions, so even people who say "I don't do politics" live with the results every day.
fact: A debate is a discussion where people give reasons for different sides; hearing good reasons and changing your mind is a strength, not a weakness.
fact: Values are the things a person believes matter most, like fairness or freedom; different values lead people to want different answers.
fact: A politician's record is what they have actually done; some politicians break promises and some keep them, so it is better to check each one than to say they are all the same.
fact: Some people say the bigger group should always win a vote; others say a smaller group should sometimes get its turn, or it will always lose.
exercise: What is politics? | How a group decides things everyone then has to live with | Only what happens inside one big building in the capital city | Shouting matches on TV between people in suits | A school subject about kings who lived long ago
exercise: Two games want the only football at lunch, and the class votes. Why did the teacher call it politics? | The group made a shared decision about something they could not all have | Because a teacher was in charge of the playground | Because football is played between two teams | Because shouting always counts as politics
exercise: Mia's family ends up at a beach near a theme park. What is that kind of deal called? | A compromise, where each side gets part of what it wants | A landslide, where one side wins the vote by a huge amount | A protest, where people gather together to demand a change | A veto, where one person blocks the thing the rest all want
exercise: The council closed the pool and kept the library. What does this show? | Spending money on one thing means less for something else | Councils have unlimited money to spend on every single building | Swimming pools are always cheaper than libraries | Nobody decides how town money gets spent
exercise: What do taxes pay for? | Shared things like schools, hospitals, roads and police | Only the wages of the king and the royal family and their staff | Only the politicians' own houses and cars | Nothing; taxes are thrown away each year
exercise: Why does tax make people so cross? | People disagree about how much to pay and who pays most | Tax is a new idea that started last year | Only children pay tax, and they hate it | Tax money always ends up being sent away to other countries
exercise: Priya wins the school council vote over Liam, who promised longer break. What probably helped her? | People thought her promise could really happen | She promised the biggest and most exciting thing of anyone | She was the loudest and most popular person in the class | The teachers counted all of her votes twice by mistake
exercise: Noah asks who made the parking rule his mum hates. What is the honest answer? | People chosen by voters made it, and voters can push to change it | It was always there, like the weather, and no person ever made it up | The traffic wardens invent fines each morning | Shops make parking rules to sell more things
exercise: Chloe sees people with signs chanting outside a big building. What are they most likely doing? | A peaceful protest to show they want something changed | Breaking the law just by standing there together in a group | Waiting in a long queue to vote for a brand new president | Rehearsing a play that will be shown on the evening news
exercise: Oisin's grandad "doesn't do politics" but moans about bins, bus fares and milk prices. What is true? | Those are all touched by political decisions, so he does care | Bins and buses have nothing at all to do with politics or voting | Only people on TV are allowed to talk about prices | Grandads cannot have opinions on politics
exercise: Kai changes his vote after hearing Grace's reason. Was that a good thing? | Yes, being persuaded by a good reason is a strength | No, changing your mind always shows that you are weak | No, you must never listen to anyone just before a vote | Yes, but only because Grace happens to be his best friend
exercise: Why do grown-ups get so heated about politics? | It decides money, safety and fairness, which people care about deeply | Politicians are paid to make everyone angry | Grown-ups simply enjoy shouting more than children | It only happens when the TV is too loud
exercise: A man on YouTube says, "All politicians are liars." What is the wisest response? | Some do lie, so check what each one actually did | He is right, so there is no point checking anything | He is wrong, because no politician has ever lied | Believe him, because YouTube videos are always checked
exercise: What are values? | The things a person believes matter most, like fairness or freedom | The prices written on things in a shop | The number of votes a person has won | The rules a teacher writes on the board
exercise: In a vote, what do some people say about a small group that always loses? | It should sometimes get a turn, or it will always lose | It should be thrown out of the group for good to keep the peace | It should be handed every single decision from now on instead | It does not exist, because votes always end up as a tie
test: Your cousin goes, "politics is just old blokes in suits yelling on telly, nothing to do with us lot." What's the best reply? | It's any group deciding things together, from class votes to bus fares | He's right, politics only happens inside Parliament | He's right, but only after you turn eighteen | Politics is just a TV programme, like the news
test: Mum's mate goes, "the council's well tight, they shut the pool to keep the library." What does "tight" mean here, and what is going on? | Short of money, so choosing one thing meant giving up another | Very strict, so they ban swimming for fun | Very busy, so they forgot to open the pool | Very rich, so they decided to go and buy a second library as well
test: Your little brother says, "If we vote and the most people win, the small group can never complain." Which is the fairest answer? | They can still give reasons, and some say small groups deserve a turn sometimes | Correct, losers in a vote must never speak again | Correct, and the small group should leave the class | Wrong, the smallest group always wins a vote
test: In a group chat a kid writes, "no cap, protesting = going to jail lol." ("No cap" means "no lie, honestly".) Is he right? | No, peaceful protest is allowed in many countries | Yes, anyone who holds a sign is arrested | Yes, but only if it is raining | No, because protests are only allowed for children
test: Uncle Dave, a proper Scouser (from Liverpool), says, "Taxes? Boss idea, la, that's what fixed me knee in the ozzy." ("Boss" means great; "ozzy" means hospital.) What is he saying? | Taxes are good because they pay for things like hospitals | Taxes should be paid only by the boss at work | Hospitals are paid for by bosses, not by taxes | His knee was fixed for free, so taxes must be pointless for him
test: What would happen if a class had one football and nobody had any way of deciding who gets it? | Arguments would go on, and the strongest might just grab it | Everyone would agree straight away, without even needing to talk | The football would somehow split itself into two separate balls | The teacher would have to buy a brand new ball for every child
test: Your nan's mate goes, "back in my day we didn't need politics, folk just got on with it." What is the best answer? | Folk still had to decide things together, so politics was there all along | She is right, politics was invented after she grew up | She is right, but only for people in big cities | Politics only began when the internet was invented
test: Gran says Kai is "delulu" (deluded) for changing his vote after Grace spoke. Who is right? | Kai, because changing your mind for a good reason is sensible | Gran, because a vote should never change after the first idea | Gran, because listening before a vote is against the rules | Neither, because a class vote cannot have reasons
test: A cartoon on telly shows a mayor deciding everything alone by flipping a coin. What's wrong with this picture of politics? | Real decisions involve people giving reasons, arguing and often voting | Nothing, real mayors always flip a coin | Mayors are not allowed to own coins | Coins can only be flipped by the king
test: Your dad, mad at the parking ticket, says, "Rules just fall out of the sky." What is true? | People made the rule, and people can work to change it | He is right, rules just appear on their own, like rain does | Only the police can ever change a rule | A rule can never be changed once it is made
test: Your mum says politics is about money, safety and fairness. Two neighbours argue about one thing for an hour and still say goodbye nicely. What does this show? | You can disagree strongly and still be kind | People who disagree must stop speaking forever | The neighbours were not really disagreeing | Being kind means you must agree with everyone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the activity designated "politics"? | The process whereby a group arrives at decisions binding upon all its members | The exclusive conduct of affairs within a national legislature | The broadcasting of disputes between public figures for the purposes of entertainment | The study of monarchs of former centuries
exam: Given that a municipal authority possesses a finite budget, identify the necessary consequence of an increase in expenditure upon one service. | A corresponding reduction in the sums available for other services | An automatic and corresponding increase in the total budget of the authority | The abolition of all taxation within the municipality | No consequence whatsoever for any other service
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of laws in a democracy. | Laws are made by representatives chosen by voters and may be amended or repealed | Laws arise spontaneously and are incapable of alteration | Laws are determined exclusively by officers of the police | Laws are composed by commercial enterprises for their own profit
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with sound reasoning in respect of the assertion that all politicians are untruthful. | Examination of the record of each individual politician | Unqualified acceptance of the assertion as stated | Unqualified rejection of any possibility of untruthfulness | Abstention from any consideration of public affairs
